    <h1>Graphs, and How to Store Them</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">Trees let each node have one parent. Graphs remove that rule: anything can connect to anything.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>say what a graph is and give two ways it differs from a tree;</li>
        <li>point out a vertex's neighbors, degree (in- and out-degree for arrows), a path, a cycle, and a loop in a drawing;</li>
        <li>tell from a drawing or a matrix whether a graph is <b>directed</b> and whether it is <b>weighted</b>;</li>
        <li>write the <b>adjacency matrix</b> and <b>adjacency list</b> of a small graph, and draw a graph from either one;</li>
        <li>choose the matrix or the list for a given graph and explain why, and read Java code that stores a graph both ways.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the linked lists lesson (for adjacency lists), the trees lessons, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- 1 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="what">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>What is a graph?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Graph</p>
        <p>A <b>graph</b> is a non-linear data structure made of a set of <b>vertices</b> (also called nodes) and a set of <b>edges</b>. Each edge connects two vertices.</p>
      </div>
      <GraphFig g={small} label="A small graph: A connects to B and D; B connects to C and D" caption="Vertices: A, B, C, D   ·   Edges: A–B, A–D, B–C, B–D" />
      <details class="reveal"><summary>Extra: how books write a graph</summary><div class="flow">
        <p class="xtra-note"><b>Extra topic.</b> You may see this short math form in books. Open this if you are curious.</p>
        <p>Books often write a graph as <b>G = (V, E)</b>: V is the set of vertices, E is the set of edges. Here, a <b>set</b> just means a group of items with no repeats, listed inside curly braces &#123; &#125;. (Sets get their own lesson later.) The graph above is V = &#123;A, B, C, D&#125; and E = &#123;A–B, A–D, B–C, B–D&#125;.</p>
      </div></details>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Graphs vs. trees</h3>
        <p>You already know one kind of graph: a <b>tree</b> is a graph with special rules (one root, every other node has exactly one parent, no cycles). A general graph drops those rules:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Tree</th><th>Graph</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Root?</b></td><td>yes, one</td><td>no root</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Parents?</b></td><td>every node except the root has exactly one</td><td>no parent/child idea; a vertex can connect to any number of others</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Cycles?</b></td><td>never</td><td>allowed (you can go around and come back)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Connected?</b></td><td>always</td><td>not always; some parts can be separate</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>So: <b>every tree is a graph, but not every graph is a tree.</b></p>
      </div>

      <div class="remember flow">
        <p class="note-label">Where you see graphs in IT</p>
        <p>Graphs are everywhere in IT. Here are four examples. Each one has vertices (the things) and edges (the connections).</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={graphsInIT()} /><figcaption class="gi-cap">
          <p>Here are a few things to notice in the pictures.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Social media.</b> You are not friends with Bea yet (the dashed line). But two of your friends are, so the app may suggest her in "People you may know."</li>
            <li><b>Maps.</b> The numbers on the roads are travel minutes. A graph with numbers on its edges is called a <b>weighted</b> graph.</li>
            <li><b>The web.</b> A link takes you from one page to another, but not back. Home links to News, but News has no link back to Home. That is why the edges are arrows. A graph with arrows is called a <b>directed</b> graph.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>You will learn both words in Part 2.</p>
        </figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="words">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Vocabulary</p>
      <h2>Graph terminology</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>These are the basic terms used with graphs. The examples refer to the small graph above. <b>Tap any row</b> to see it in the graph (on a computer, just point at the row).</p>
      <div class="terms-ref">
        <aside class="tr-side" aria-label="The small graph, for reference">
          <GraphFig g={small} label="The small graph: A connects to B and D; B connects to C and D" />
          <p class="tr-note small">Orange = the term. Gold = the related vertices.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="tr-main scroll-x">
          <table class="tbl tr-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Vertex</b> (node)</td><td>one item in the graph</td><td>A</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,B" data-hl2="" data-e="A-B"><td><b>Edge</b></td><td>a connection between two vertices</td><td>A–B</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="B,C" data-hl2="" data-e="B-C"><td><b>Adjacent</b> (neighbors)</td><td>two vertices joined by an edge</td><td>B and C are adjacent; A and C are not</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="B" data-hl2="A,C,D" data-e="A-B,B-C,B-D"><td><b>Degree</b></td><td>the number of edges touching a vertex</td><td>B has degree 3</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,C" data-hl2="B" data-e="A-B,B-C"><td><b>Path</b></td><td>a sequence of vertices, each joined to the next by an edge</td><td>A → B → C</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A" data-hl2="B,D" data-e="A-B,B-D,A-D"><td><b>Cycle</b></td><td>a path that starts and ends at the same vertex, without repeating any edge or any other vertex</td><td>A → B → D → A</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="" data-hl2="" data-e=""><td><b>Loop</b></td><td>an edge from a vertex to itself</td><td>(none here)</td></tr>
            <tr tabindex="0" data-hl="A,B,C,D" data-hl2="" data-e="A-B,A-D,B-C,B-D"><td><b>Connected</b></td><td>every vertex can be reached from every other vertex</td><td>this graph is connected</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Directed or undirected?</h3>
        <p>Graphs come in two kinds, depending on whether the edges have a direction.</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="kind-tag kt-undir">Undirected</p>
            <GraphFig g={small} label="Undirected graph" />
            <p class="small">Edges have <b>no direction</b>. A–B means you can go A to B <i>and</i> B to A. Like Facebook friends: if you are my friend, I am yours.</p>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="kind-tag kt-dir">Directed</p>
            <GraphFig g={smallDir} label="Directed graph with arrows" />
            <p class="small">Each edge is an <b>arrow</b>. A → B lets you go from A to B, but not back. Like following someone on TikTok. They do not have to follow you back.</p>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>In a directed graph, degree splits in two. The <b>out-degree</b> counts arrows leaving a vertex, and the <b>in-degree</b> counts arrows coming in. Here is vertex A:</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="kind-tag kt-out">Out-degree</p>
            <GraphFig g={smallDir} nodeMarks={{ A: 'current', B: 'seen', D: 'seen' }} edgeMarks={{ 'A-B': 'path', 'A-D': 'path' }} label="Directed graph: the arrows A to B and A to D, leaving A, are highlighted" />
            <p class="small">Count the <b>arrows leaving A</b> (highlighted), A → B and A → D. That makes 2, so the out-degree of A is 2. In TikTok terms, this is how many accounts A <b>follows</b>.</p>
          </figure>
          <figure class="fig">
            <p class="kind-tag kt-in">In-degree</p>
            <GraphFig g={smallDir} nodeMarks={{ A: 'current', C: 'seen' }} edgeMarks={{ 'C-A': 'tree' }} label="Directed graph: the arrow C to A, coming into A, is highlighted" />
            <p class="small">Count the <b>arrows coming into A</b> (highlighted). There is only C → A, so the in-degree of A is 1. In TikTok terms, this is how many <b>followers</b> A has.</p>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p><b>Tip.</b> If the arrowhead touches the vertex, it counts as <b>in</b>. If the tail starts there, it counts as <b>out</b>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Weighted or unweighted?</h3>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="two-col">
            <GraphFig g={weighted} label="Weighted graph with numbers on the edges" />
            <div class="flow">
              <p>In a <span class="term-chip tc-w">weighted</span> graph, each edge carries a number called its <b>weight</b>, such as distance, cost, or time. In an <span class="term-chip tc-u">unweighted</span> graph, every edge counts the same.</p>
              <p>Here the weights are made-up travel minutes. A → D → B → C takes 4 + 5 + 12 = 21, but A → B → C takes 7 + 12 = 19.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <div class="remember">
          <p class="note-label">Where you see this in real life</p>
          <p>Google Maps and Waze store places as vertices and road segments as edges (a one-way street is a directed edge). The weight is the expected travel time, which changes with traffic. The app picks the path with the <b>smallest total weight</b>, so "shortest" usually means <b>fastest</b>.</p>
          <figure class="fig illus-fig illus map-app-fig"><Fragment set:html={mapAppGraph()} /><figcaption>A made-up map (top) and the weighted graph it stores (bottom). The blue route is A → B → C, 19 minutes.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Loops</h3>
        <GraphFig g={withLoop} label="Directed graph where C has an arrow to itself" />
        <p>C has a <b>loop</b>. A loop is an edge that leaves a vertex and comes right back to the <b>same</b> vertex.</p>
        <p>Think of a web page that links to itself, like a "Back to top" link. The page is the vertex, and the link is an edge that points back to the same page.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={loopPagePic()} /></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="matrix">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Storing a graph, way 1</p>
      <h2>Adjacency matrix</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>The graph drawings you have been seeing so far are for human eyes and understanding. But a computer does not store data in those drawings. It stores only the <b>connections</b>, in one of two ways. These are the <span class="term-chip tc-mx">adjacency matrix</span> (this part) and the <span class="term-chip tc-ls">adjacency list</span> (Part 4).</p>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Adjacency matrix</p>
        <p>An <b>adjacency matrix</b> is a 2D array with one row and one column for each vertex. The cell <b>[row][column]</b> is <b>1</b> if there is an edge <b>from</b> the row vertex <b>to</b> the column vertex, and <b>0</b> if there is not.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="two-d">
        <h3>What is a 2D array?</h3>
        <p>A <b>2D array</b> is a grid of slots, like a classroom seat plan. Each slot has two indexes, <b>[row][column]</b>, both counting from 0. (The Java code is in <a href="#java">Part 8</a>.)</p>
        <div class="g2-wrap">
          <span class="g2-collab" aria-hidden="true">column →</span>
          <span class="g2-rowlab" aria-hidden="true">row ↓</span>
        <table class="grid2d" aria-label="A 2D array named seats with 3 rows and 4 columns. Every slot holds 0 except seats[1][2], row 1, column 2, which holds 7.">
          <thead><tr><th class="g2-corner"></th>{[0, 1, 2, 3].map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>{[0, 1, 2].map((r) => <tr><th>{r}</th>{[0, 1, 2, 3].map((c) => <td class={r === 1 && c === 2 ? 'hot' : ''}>{r === 1 && c === 2 ? 7 : 0}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap g2-cap"><code>seats[1][2]</code> is row 1, column 2 (orange). Row first, then column.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Think of a friends chart</p>
        <p>Ana, Ben, Cris, and Dan want to record who is friends with whom, so they make a chart on paper.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Write the four names down the left side, then again across the top.</li>
          <li>Each box is where one row meets one column. It asks one question: is the person in this row friends with the person in this column?</li>
          <li>Write 1 for yes and 0 for no.</li>
        </ol>
        <figure class="fig"><Fragment set:html={friendsChart()} /></figure>
        <p>Read Ana's row across: she is friends with Ben and Dan, so her row is <span class="mono">0 1 0 1</span>. Friendship goes both ways, so the Ana–Ben friendship fills <b>two</b> boxes (orange): row Ana, column Ben, and row Ben, column Ana.</p>
        <p>This chart <b>is</b> an adjacency matrix. The classmates are the vertices, and the friendships are the edges.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig mxtap-box how-tabs" data-tabs>
        <p class="fig-cap">What does each box in the matrix mean?</p>
        <div class="btn-row how-tab-row" role="tablist" aria-label="Kind of graph">
          <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="tu" aria-selected="true">Undirected</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="td" aria-selected="false">Directed</button>
        </div>
        <div data-pane="tu" role="tabpanel"><MatrixTap id="mxtap" g={small} bare hint="Each box tells you if the vertex in its row and the vertex in its column are connected. 1 means yes, and 0 means no. Tap a box to check it on the drawing." /></div>
        <div data-pane="td" role="tabpanel" hidden><MatrixTap id="mxtap-d" g={smallDir} bare hint="Each box tells you if there is an arrow going from the vertex in its row to the vertex in its column. 1 means yes, and 0 means no. Tap a box to check it on the drawing." /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="plain" data-toc="How to write the matrix">
        <p class="note-label">How to write the matrix from a drawing</p>
        <p>There are four steps. Pick the kind of graph, then go through the steps one at a time.</p>
        <div class="how-tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="btn-row how-tab-row" role="tablist" aria-label="Kind of graph">
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="u" aria-selected="true">Undirected</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="d" aria-selected="false">Directed</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="w" aria-selected="false">Weighted undirected</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="wd" aria-selected="false">Weighted directed</button>
          </div>
          <div data-pane="u" role="tabpanel"><StepShow id="matrix-how-u" cap="Undirected graph (the friends chart graph)" hint="Each edge and its two boxes share the same color." steps={matrixHowSteps(small)} /></div>
          <div data-pane="d" role="tabpanel" hidden><StepShow id="matrix-how-d" cap="Directed graph (from Part 2)" hint="Each arrow and its one box share the same color." steps={matrixHowSteps(smallDir)} /></div>
          <div data-pane="w" role="tabpanel" hidden><StepShow id="matrix-how-w" cap="Weighted undirected graph (the travel-time graph from Part 2)" hint="Each edge and its two boxes share the same color." steps={matrixHowSteps(weighted)} /></div>
          <div data-pane="wd" role="tabpanel" hidden><StepShow id="matrix-how-wd" cap="Weighted directed graph (the directed graph from Part 2, with weights)" hint="Each arrow and its one box share the same color." steps={matrixHowSteps(weightedDir)} /></div>
        </div>
        <p class="small">Inside the computer, A is index 0, B is index 1, and so on. The letters are only labels.</p>
      </div>
      <h3 id="most">Which vertex has the most connections?</h3>
      <p>Some questions ask which vertex has the most edges. You can answer straight from the matrix.</p>
      <div class="rule-box"><span class="rule-tag">Rule</span><p>Look for the <b>column</b> with the <b>most 1s</b>.</p></div>
      <div class="cmp-two cnt-two">
        <div class="cmp-side">
          <p class="kind-tag kt-undir">Undirected graph</p>
          <GraphFig g={small} label="The undirected graph from the friends chart" />
          <Fragment set:html={countMx(small, false)} />
          <p class="small">Count the 1s in each column. Column B has the most, <b>3</b>, so B has the most connections (B has degree 3). In an undirected graph, row B gives the same count, because the matrix is a mirror image.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cmp-side">
          <p class="kind-tag kt-dir">Directed graph</p>
          <GraphFig g={smallDir} label="The directed graph from above" />
          <Fragment set:html={countMx(smallDir, true)} />
          <p class="small">A column counts the arrows <b>coming in</b> (the in-degree). Column C has the most, <b>2</b>, so C has the most arrows coming in (from B and from D).</p>
          <p class="small">A row counts the arrows <b>going out</b> (the out-degree), shown in the "out" column. Row A has the most, <b>2</b>, so A has the most arrows going out (to B and to D).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Practice all of this in the <a href="#read">practice problems</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="list">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Storing a graph, way 2</p>
      <h2>Adjacency list</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Adjacency list</p>
        <p>An <b>adjacency list</b> gives each vertex its own list of the vertices it has an edge to. The lists are often linked lists, so each one ends with <code>null</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <GraphFig g={smallDir} show={['graph', 'list']} label="The directed graph and its adjacency list" />
      <p>This should look familiar. Each row uses a <Peek label="linked list" title="From the Linked lists lesson" html={llPeek} /> from the linked lists lesson. But look closely at where each list <b>starts</b>.</p>
      <div class="hn-fig" aria-label="A's row: the vertex A, then its list B, D, null">
        <span class="adjlist hn-head"><span class="head">A</span></span>
        <span class="hn-arr">→</span>
        <span class="adjlist hn-list"><span class="cell"><b>B</b><i></i></span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="cell"><b>D</b><i></i></span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="null">null</span></span>
        <p class="hn-lbl hn-lbl-a">The <b>vertex</b>, kept in an array.<br><b class="hn-not">Not part of the list.</b></p>
        <p class="hn-lbl hn-lbl-l"><b>A's linked list.</b><br>It starts at the <b>first neighbor</b>, B.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="hn-key">The green-filled box only <b>points to</b> where the list starts. The list itself is <span class="mono">B → D → null</span>. It has <b>two boxes</b>, B and D, so A has edges to B and D.</p>
      <div class="plain" data-toc="How to write the list">
        <p class="note-label">How to write an adjacency list from a drawing</p>
        <p>There are two steps. Pick the kind of graph, then go through the steps one at a time.</p>
        <div class="how-tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="btn-row how-tab-row" role="tablist" aria-label="Kind of graph">
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="u" aria-selected="true">Undirected</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="d" aria-selected="false">Directed</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="w" aria-selected="false">Weighted undirected</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="wd" aria-selected="false">Weighted directed</button>
          </div>
          <div data-pane="u" role="tabpanel"><StepShow id="list-how-u" cap="How to write an adjacency list: undirected graph" hint="Orange = the vertex you are on and its edges." steps={listHowSteps(small)} /></div>
          <div data-pane="d" role="tabpanel" hidden><StepShow id="list-how-d" cap="How to write an adjacency list: directed graph" hint="Orange = the vertex you are on and the arrows that start there." steps={listHowSteps(smallDir)} /></div>
          <div data-pane="w" role="tabpanel" hidden><StepShow id="list-how-w" cap="How to write an adjacency list: weighted undirected graph" hint="Orange = the vertex you are on and its edges." steps={listHowSteps(weighted)} /></div>
          <div data-pane="wd" role="tabpanel" hidden><StepShow id="list-how-wd" cap="How to write an adjacency list: weighted directed graph" hint="Orange = the vertex you are on and the arrows that start there." steps={listHowSteps(weightedDir)} /></div>
        </div>
        <script is:inline>
          document.querySelectorAll('[data-tabs]').forEach(function (box) {
            var tabs = box.querySelectorAll('[data-tab]');
            tabs.forEach(function (t) {
              t.addEventListener('click', function () {
                tabs.forEach(function (x) { x.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(x === t)); });
                box.querySelectorAll('[data-pane]').forEach(function (p) { p.hidden = p.dataset.pane !== t.dataset.tab; });
              });
            });
          });
        </script>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="build">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Play</p>
      <h2>Build your own</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Here, one graph is shown three ways at once: as a drawing, a matrix, and a list. Change the matrix and watch the other two follow.</p>
      <MatrixLab id="mxlab" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="compare">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Choosing</p>
      <h2>Matrix or list?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Both ways store the same graph. They differ in how much memory they use and in how fast they answer two common questions. The three cards below compare them.</p>

      <div class="paper flow cmp-q">
        <h3>1. Which one uses less memory?</h3>
        <p>It depends on how many edges the graph has. Keep in mind that a <span class="term-chip tc-ls">list box</span> <b>is bigger than</b> a <span class="term-chip tc-mx">matrix box</span>. A <span class="term-chip tc-mx">matrix box</span> holds just one number. A <span class="term-chip tc-ls">list box</span> holds a vertex <b>and</b> a link to the next box (the small square on its right).</p>
        <div class="how-tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="btn-row how-tab-row" role="tablist" aria-label="How many edges">
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="few" aria-selected="true">Few edges</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" role="tab" data-tab="many" aria-selected="false">Many edges</button>
          </div>
          <div data-pane="few" role="tabpanel" class="flow">
            <div class="cmp-gline"><GraphFig g={sparse6} w={340} h={280} label="Graph with 6 vertices: A–C, A–E, B–F, D–E" /><p>Here the 6 vertices have only <b>4 edges</b>. Most pairs of vertices are not connected.</p></div>
            <div class="cmp-two cmp-rows">
              <div class="cmp-side cmp-dim0"><p class="cmp-lbl">Matrix</p><p class="bk-line">Each <span class="term-chip tc-mx">matrix box</span> is one <b>pair of vertices</b>. A 1 means that pair has an <b>edge</b>.</p><Fragment set:html={mxNoCap(matrixHtml(sparse6, cellsOf(sparse6)))} /><p class="small">A matrix <b>always</b> stores every box, even when there is no edge. Here that is 6 × 6 = <span class="term-chip tc-mx">36 matrix boxes</span>, but only 8 of them hold a 1.</p><p class="small cmp-key"><b>28 grey boxes hold 0.</b> They use memory, yet they store nothing.</p></div>
              <div class="cmp-side"><p class="cmp-lbl">List</p><p class="bk-line">Each white <span class="term-chip tc-ls">list box</span> is one <b>neighbor</b> of the vertex in the green-filled box.</p><Fragment set:html={listHtml(sparse6)} /><div class="cmp-count"><p class="small">Count the white boxes in each row. That gives only <span class="term-chip tc-ls">8 list boxes</span>.</p><p class="tally"><span class="tc"><b>A</b>2</span><span class="tc"><b>B</b>1</span><span class="tc"><b>C</b>1</span><span class="tc"><b>D</b>1</span><span class="tc"><b>E</b>2</span><span class="tc"><b>F</b>1</span></p></div><p class="small cmp-side-note"><b>Green-filled boxes and null are not counted.</b> They are not part of the linked lists.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="cmp-win"><b>The list uses less memory.</b> <span class="term-chip tc-ls">8 list boxes</span> are far less than <span class="term-chip tc-mx">36 matrix boxes</span>, even though list boxes are bigger.</p>
          </div>
          <div data-pane="many" role="tabpanel" class="flow" hidden>
            <div class="cmp-gline"><GraphFig g={dense6} w={340} h={280} label="The same 6 vertices, now with every vertex connected to every other vertex" /><p>The <b>same 6 vertices</b>, but now every vertex is connected to every other vertex. That makes <b>15 edges</b>.</p></div>
            <div class="cmp-two cmp-rows">
              <div class="cmp-side cmp-dim0"><p class="cmp-lbl">Matrix</p><p class="bk-line">Each <span class="term-chip tc-mx">matrix box</span> is one <b>pair of vertices</b>. A 1 means that pair has an <b>edge</b>.</p><Fragment set:html={mxNoCap(matrixHtml(dense6, cellsOf(dense6)))} /><p class="small">A matrix <b>always</b> stores every box. Here that is still 6 × 6 = <span class="term-chip tc-mx">36 matrix boxes</span>, and now 30 of them hold a 1.</p><p class="small cmp-key"><b>Only 6 grey boxes hold 0.</b> Almost nothing is wasted.</p></div>
              <div class="cmp-side cmp-dense"><p class="cmp-lbl">List</p><p class="bk-line">Each white <span class="term-chip tc-ls">list box</span> is one <b>neighbor</b> of the vertex in the green-filled box.</p><Fragment set:html={listHtml(dense6)} /><div class="cmp-count"><p class="small">Count the white boxes in each row. That gives <span class="term-chip tc-ls">30 list boxes</span>.</p><p class="tally"><span class="tc"><b>A</b>5</span><span class="tc"><b>B</b>5</span><span class="tc"><b>C</b>5</span><span class="tc"><b>D</b>5</span><span class="tc"><b>E</b>5</span><span class="tc"><b>F</b>5</span></p></div><p class="small cmp-side-note"><b>Each <span class="term-chip tc-ls">list box</span> is bigger.</b> It holds a vertex and a link, so <span class="term-chip tc-ls">30 list boxes</span> take more memory than the matrix's 36.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="cmp-win"><b>The matrix uses less memory.</b> In Java, one <span class="term-chip tc-ls">list box</span> takes several times the memory of one <span class="term-chip tc-mx">matrix box</span>, so <span class="term-chip tc-ls">30 list boxes</span> take more space than <span class="term-chip tc-mx">36 matrix boxes</span>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="paper flow cmp-q">
        <h3>2. Which one tells you faster if two vertices are connected?</h3>
        <div class="cmp-ex"><span class="cmp-ex-tag">Example</span><p>Are <span class="vchip">A</span> and <span class="vchip">E</span> connected?</p></div>
        <GraphFig g={sparse6} w={340} h={280} nodeMarks={{ A: 'current', E: 'current' }} edgeMarks={{ 'A-E': 'path' }} label="Graph with 6 vertices: A–C, A–E, B–F, D–E. A and E are highlighted." />
        <div class="cmp-two">
          <div class="cmp-side"><p class="cmp-lbl">Matrix</p><Fragment set:html={mxNoCap(matrixHtml(sparse6, cellsOf(sparse6), ['A-E']))} /><p class="small">You just go to the box where <b>row A meets column E</b>. You immediately know where to go. It holds 1, so A and E are connected.</p></div>
          <div class="cmp-side"><p class="cmp-lbl">List</p><Fragment set:html={listHtml(sparse6, null, 'A', ['A-C', 'A-E'])} /><p class="small">There is no single box to represent the connection between A and E. You have to go to <b>A's list</b> and check each box from the start until you find E (or reach null). A longer list means more checking.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="cmp-win"><b>The matrix is faster.</b> It always takes one box.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="paper flow cmp-q">
        <h3>3. Which one finds a vertex's neighbors faster?</h3>
        <div class="cmp-ex"><span class="cmp-ex-tag">Example</span><p>What are <span class="vchip">A</span>'s neighbors?</p></div>
        <GraphFig g={sparse6} w={340} h={280} nodeMarks={{ A: 'current', C: 'seen', E: 'seen' }} edgeMarks={{ 'A-C': 'path', 'A-E': 'path' }} label="Graph with 6 vertices: A–C, A–E, B–F, D–E. A and its neighbors C and E are highlighted." />
        <div class="cmp-two">
          <div class="cmp-side"><p class="cmp-lbl">Matrix</p><Fragment set:html={mxNoCap(matrixHtml(sparse6, cellsOf(sparse6), ['A-A','A-B','A-C','A-D','A-E','A-F'], 'A'))} /><p class="small">You have to go through <b>row A</b> and read <b>every box</b> in it. A 1 means "neighbor," and a 0 means "not a neighbor." The 1s are under C and E. You read <b>6 boxes</b>, but only 2 of them matter.</p></div>
          <div class="cmp-side"><p class="cmp-lbl">List</p><Fragment set:html={listHtml(sparse6, null, 'A', ['A-C', 'A-E'])} /><p class="small">You just go to <b>A's list</b>, where everything listed in it is that vertex's neighbor. A's neighbors are C and E. You read only <b>2 boxes</b>. Vertices that are not neighbors are never stored.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="cmp-win"><b>The list is faster.</b> It has no 0s to skip.</p>
      </div>
      <script is:inline>
        document.querySelectorAll('#compare [data-tabs]').forEach(function (box) {
          var tabs = box.querySelectorAll('[data-tab]');
          tabs.forEach(function (t) { t.addEventListener('click', function () {
            tabs.forEach(function (x) { x.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(x === t)); });
            box.querySelectorAll('[data-pane]').forEach(function (p) { p.hidden = p.dataset.pane !== t.dataset.tab; });
          }); });
        });
      </script>

      <h3 class="cmp-which">So which one should you use?</h3>
      <p>It depends on how many edges the graph has. A graph with many edges is called <b>dense</b>. A graph with only a few edges is called <b>sparse</b>.</p>
      <div class="cmp-two cmp-graphs">
        <figure class="fig"><Fragment set:html={svgGraph(dense5, { label: 'Dense graph: every vertex is connected to every other vertex', w: 300, h: 270 })} /><figcaption><span class="kind-tag kt-dense">Dense graph</span>Most boxes in its matrix would be 1, so no space is wasted. Use a <b>matrix</b>.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><Fragment set:html={svgGraph(sparse5, { label: 'Sparse graph: only three edges', w: 300, h: 270 })} /><figcaption><span class="kind-tag kt-sparse">Sparse graph</span>Most boxes in its matrix would be 0. Use a <b>list</b>.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of your class</p>
        <p>Say you want to record who is friends with whom in a class of <b>40 students</b>.</p>
        <p>With a <b>matrix</b>, you need a 40 × 40 grid. That is <b>1,600 boxes</b>, one for every pair of students. But each student is friends with only a few classmates, so almost all of the boxes would be 0.</p>
        <p>With a <b>list</b>, each student writes only their friends' names on an index card. Nothing is written for classmates who are not their friends.</p>
        <div class="cls-pics">
          <figure><p class="cls-big"><span>Matrix</span><b>1,600</b> boxes</p><Fragment set:html={classPic.m} /><figcaption>Only the {classPic.ones} <b class="cls-or">orange</b> boxes hold a 1. All the grey boxes hold 0.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><p class="cls-big"><span>List</span><b>{classPic.ones}</b> boxes</p><Fragment set:html={classPic.l} /><figcaption>One <b class="cls-or">orange</b> box per friend. Nothing for the rest.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <p>Most real graphs are like your class. In a social network or a map of roads, each point connects to only a few others. These graphs are sparse, so lists are used more often.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="read">
    <div class="chapter-head">
